UPDATE pm_learning_lessons SET content = '## Seventeen people, one weekend, four sentences\n\n![Snowbird ski resort, Utah, where the Agile Manifesto was written in February 2001](https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/fe/May_28_2006_Snowbird_UT_USA.jpg)\n\nIn February 2001, seventeen software practitioners met at a ski lodge in Snowbird, Utah — among them Kent Beck, Martin Fowler, Ward Cunningham, Ron Jeffries, Jeff Sutherland, Ken Schwaber. They weren\'t there to build a framework. They were comparing notes on lighter-weight ways of working that all seemed to be converging on the same thing, and by the end of the weekend they\'d written it down as four short value statements:\n\n> Individuals and interactions over processes and tools\n> Working software over comprehensive documentation\n> Customer collaboration over contract negotiation\n> Responding to change over following a plan\n\n> That is, while there is value in the items on the right, we value the items on the left more.\n\nThat last line matters. This isn\'t \"processes and tools are bad\" — it\'s a statement of priority under pressure, for when the two pull against each other.\n\n## What the Manifesto was actually rebelling against\n\nThe dominant way of running software projects at the time — like most projects, then and now — was heavyweight and document-led: gather complete requirements up front, produce a detailed specification, get it signed off, then build to the spec — often taking a year or more before anyone outside the project saw working software. Change was treated as a failure of the original requirements-gathering, to be minimised through change control rather than expected and welcomed. Industry failure-rate studies from the 1990s (the Standish Group\'s CHAOS reports, whatever you make of their exact numbers) were widely cited at the time as evidence that this approach simply didn\'t work often enough.\n\nThe twelve principles that followed the four values spell out what to do instead: deliver working software frequently (\"a couple of weeks to a couple of months, with a preference to the shorter timescale\"), welcome changing requirements \"even late in development,\" and — principle 12 — \"at regular intervals, the team reflects on how to become more effective, then tunes and adjusts its behaviour accordingly.\" That last one is the retrospective, written into the founding document itself.\n\n## A short document making a big claim\n\nRead quickly, this looks like practical advice for software teams. Read carefully, it\'s staking out a position on how good outcomes actually come about: not by getting the plan right in advance and executing faithfully, but by building something small, testing it against reality, and adjusting — repeatedly, as a discipline, not as an exception. That\'s a much older argument than 2001, and tracing where it actually comes from is what the rest of this course does.\n\nNotice, too, who \"reality\" actually means here. Principle one is to satisfy the customer through early and continuous delivery, and \"customer collaboration over contract negotiation\" is one of the four values, not a footnote to the other three. Testing against reality, in this document, mostly cashes out as testing against the actual person who\'ll use the thing — checked continuously, not assumed correct and revealed only at the end. That user-first framing runs all the way through this course, right up to how a national digital service standard structures itself around it in the final lesson.\n\n## Further reading\n\n- [Manifesto for Agile Software Development](https://agilemanifesto.org/)\n- [The Twelve Principles](https://agilemanifesto.org/principles.html)'
WHERE title = 'The Agile Manifesto' AND content NOT LIKE "%Snowbird_UT_USA.jpg%";
